#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120676} :activity/id #uuid "045805f2-d7d9-501d-8e4b-541e2bb65e50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315 :topic/id #uuid "93e6a7ce-2e63-5dea-bbbb-a00575db5770" :topic/math-academy-id 1852} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121035 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3193 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "05c77385-415b-5833-b935-9be3a2038c69" :step/math-academy-id 17469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119619 :question/id #uuid "d5a81b39-421e-55c3-90de-d9f4e6489cd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4795"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaefbc53-9df9-54a0-a969-66e062553d5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421530}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "74f8be2d-0f86-52de-8277-fd7db395c80d" :step/math-academy-id 17471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121036 :tutorial/content "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/dd/ddef437718d3a11811da02a17e2207d75e2d1852eeada67ab494af45b2a655e2.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/1f/1f92378ff1d1409f0928caa4b3ce98861784d0f2aa51dd1eda30e8877a0134ff.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3194 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span"} :step/id #uuid "15713f7b-cd11-55c3-93fd-8040ac62d36f" :step/math-academy-id 17477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119620 :question/id #uuid "94a17888-dcef-58e0-8147-0dec3745f136" :question/math-academy-id "e-4799"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421528}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies on a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "33abdb88-235d-550f-b5bb-06946454437c" :step/math-academy-id 17478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119671 :question/id #uuid "21a6808a-5b1c-5b1d-9bd4-da605fb6d920" :question/math-academy-id "e-6218"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fac58a79-2d3f-5ad3-b1ab-c5ce279c4ebe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436138}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Such That a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "5cca50af-2a48-5696-bc3a-fc57e95232b0" :step/math-academy-id 23264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120678}}] :activity/title "Linear Span of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120046}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436139} :activity/id #uuid "a554ac31-b397-5970-b942-0ac30a77ef87" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315 :topic/id #uuid "93e6a7ce-2e63-5dea-bbbb-a00575db5770" :topic/math-academy-id 1852} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "711bff2a-6953-5410-bb47-66e0c9bf361e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436132} {:answer/id #uuid "94b9bad3-ee59-5063-a347-e17815281f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436133} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f39043-eded-58ee-8538-d1b96c3a350b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436134} {:answer/id #uuid "a676903a-1471-5d07-b4fb-3436e54d4e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436135} {:answer/id #uuid "caaab5ae-2e33-5881-8499-57baf2a35a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436132} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4437cfd-d611-50b5-a26a-25a34c0f8218" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1dfec754-9c19-551c-8df7-102df6435813" :question/math-academy-id "q-34636" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-8 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -18 \\\\ 8 & 2 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -18 \\\\ 0 & 10 & k-32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & k-32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-10{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-2.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-2\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19ecde81-ba5d-52b8-b7b7-9282a3f95b93" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436142}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ea0212-0c8f-50de-a9e9-986f267bdb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339138} {:answer/id #uuid "25b09d72-beeb-56f8-b3c5-bd45509568ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339451} {:answer/id #uuid "392acaa5-a047-5743-bd68-0e133d8bead9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339642} {:answer/id #uuid "c4734975-ddc9-5119-b9e9-59e873e223ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340873} {:answer/id #uuid "d55e6034-4f45-5b74-b206-75e90bc1aef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341016}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339138} :answer-field/id #uuid "690b25a1-892c-577b-b6f7-6ff437d6dcd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e12a2efc-9d88-56c5-96b5-ec530d76269d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74594" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -32 \\\\ 2 & -1 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -32 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k-18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & k-18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-6.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-6\\,⇒\\,k=6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "43f3636b-da52-552f-b6cc-fe77773f48fa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285f60c4-cfc6-509d-a8ca-df316a92348e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238408} {:answer/id #uuid "306f3ae9-9703-5c61-9e1f-a029efeb6fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238477} {:answer/id #uuid "7197f81b-8003-5f00-b98f-69a60e60b685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239030} {:answer/id #uuid "7aef6609-8c36-5411-ba86-81d4a40f69b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239115} {:answer/id #uuid "db09174f-1cdc-5bdb-8120-63756b097fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239966} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f5dbe15-3666-592c-9f7a-5c21bff9ad15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "edff4129-bf9d-5206-854e-d3e5b43ca237" :question/math-academy-id "q-105253" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc,$ $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨-1,3,2⟩$, $c=⟨2,-1,-1⟩$, and  $u=⟨5,-5,-4⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+2t=5 \\\\ 3s-t=-5 \\\\ 2s-t=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+2t=5 \\\\ t=2 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+2t & =5 \\\\ -s+2(2) & =5 \\\\ -s+4 & =5 \\\\ s & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-1,\\,t=2,$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=-b+2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-1)\\cdot 2=-2.$"} :step/id #uuid "70ef282b-5ccd-5b1e-a1f8-1bcffa1e4789" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436143}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436142 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28049f08-5684-5304-ad3d-9f534b59f279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339478} {:answer/id #uuid "67519cd7-cc6e-5952-bf35-4a4a24da747e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340043} {:answer/id #uuid "79c14b9a-0376-5405-a220-fdac02f464da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340214} {:answer/id #uuid "92c70a7d-6a91-5cb6-8d17-c5ff99a5580f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340448} {:answer/id #uuid "b383d39f-e410-5314-b906-611aa1b45702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340043} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6c41871-59a4-56ef-80fe-7d004904e3a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "62066547-f160-5da7-be83-cd71028d6d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-74556" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 6 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & k\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & k-6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-9{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-15\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=k-15.$ Therefore, the system has a solution only if\n\n$$\n0=k-15\\,⇒\\,k=15.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9b5c264f-f673-5283-81e5-4aa5642b7e81" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436143 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145063fc-8e8e-5c5e-b9c0-4ce50643aeca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238221} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd14ba1-3c05-5721-9490-57e8bf80fea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238742} {:answer/id #uuid "a01540c4-5818-561d-9151-d28aeb0e7fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239452} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e4047e-41ea-50d0-ab94-58e7363cb874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239637} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdd9058-9132-5515-90b7-33b231320d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238742} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dee8bc9-1a1f-59f0-8b66-162d23f34c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8c4cb4f7-2d57-5ca9-8ffa-a4fe4548b0de" :question/math-academy-id "q-105265" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc,$ $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨-1,-1,4⟩$, $c=⟨-3,-8,14⟩$, and  $u=⟨5,-10,-14⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-s-3t=5 \\\\ -s-8t=-10 \\\\ 4s+14t=-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -8 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 14 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -15 \\\\ 4 & 14 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-s-3t=5 \\\\ t=3 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s-3t & =5 \\\\ -s-3(3) & =5 \\\\ -s-9 & =5 \\\\ s & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=-14,\\,t=3,$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=-14b+3c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=(-14)\\cdot 3=-42.$"} :step/id #uuid "acd92085-628d-5fd7-8ceb-59efc32f4636"}] :activity/title "Linear Span of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436131}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120625} :activity/id #uuid "faea29b6-21e9-5455-abc1-ad6fe37387f0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121020 :tutorial/content "Remember the following three standard elementary row operations: \n\n  I. Swapping two rows\n\n  II. Multiplying a row by a non-zero number\n\n  III. Adding a multiple of one row to another row\n\nOf these three elementary row operations, only III (adding a multiple of one row to another row) *preserves* the determinant. The other two operations, switching two rows or multiplying a row by a non-zero number, *change* the determinant.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the matrix ![](images/fa/fa51d7c585db408c47e8640ae839c1a3bbf94e2b0097e1ade511e5773461f1e8.svg) Taking the determinant, we have\n![](images/bc/bcf5c5dc9f5b7ed3849e719665f802040cf51e3334ae99e25ea7648cbb0f6134.svg)\n\nIf we swap the two rows, we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply top row by ${\\color{red}2},$ then we get a different determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we add twice the top row to the bottom row, then we get the same determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3+2\\cdot 1 & 4+2\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5ae8e63-4d2c-5491-9908-96fe94243ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3004 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "57d74627-70e0-5830-ba95-6cb026f4de4b" :step/math-academy-id 16213 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119589 :question/id #uuid "596aa514-d598-5ffe-affe-f5580d130bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4422"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74764043-e1e7-56d6-8f53-9d287a30e538" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411967}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows"} :step/id #uuid "07d38f85-837d-577c-a3fa-ed4adb824e28" :step/math-academy-id 16214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120704}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119590 :question/id #uuid "89152146-8f04-5b4f-8436-15b82431c35d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4423"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f11b223-edd2-59b8-b761-164d56e29746" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411971}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Row by a Number"} :step/id #uuid "b2bed0ab-d12e-5d90-bbf5-94e480bceb4c" :step/math-academy-id 16215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119591 :question/id #uuid "6d46c442-4675-5e0e-a834-a8b10ad2b86a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24e5e193-5e30-5480-9e5d-b7b8f1d5de87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498488}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Applying Several Elementary Operations"} :step/id #uuid "9c7f038a-e114-5e98-bc36-49762d4031f6" :step/math-academy-id 16216 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121021 :tutorial/content "Since $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A),$ the corresponding elementary column operations on determinants have the same properties as the elementary row ones.\n\n- Swapping two columns changes the sign of the determinant.\n\n- Multiplying a column by a number scales the determinant by that number.\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column does not change the determinant." :tutorial/id #uuid "bac0b72b-b76a-5a06-9312-d2b7b8ac4247" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3005 :tutorial/title "Column Operations on Determinants"} :step/id #uuid "d5da1bd5-0cc1-56fe-a8d7-36aa6576326a" :step/math-academy-id 16217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121044 :tutorial/content "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=-det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f151b612-1bdb-5723-af50-a48439141fbc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3236 :tutorial/title "Switching Two Rows Changes the Sign of the Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "1889d210-a2bc-5893-b7e3-879d8fc8961f" :step/math-academy-id 17635 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120704 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121045 :tutorial/content "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n![](images/08/0826c05fe2eabec96065eb2c83228168129b42c309764df4b101f4a520c4a0f9.svg)\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]=k\\cdot det[\\begin{aligned}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09c28da6-5340-56f9-bbf5-427a824977ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3237 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Row By a Number Scales the Determinant By That Number"} :step/id #uuid "0cc1f27c-f5d8-5b74-9602-32ce4d87b799" :step/math-academy-id 17658 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119665 :question/id #uuid "cbd9b4bc-146a-57f7-a03f-f045f86f3c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-6143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a0ab53e-79ff-5520-bb50-069fd381d29d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483371}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Row Operation Given the Connection Between Two Determinants"} :step/id #uuid "e1ec39b0-859a-5c8a-a8ca-f8a0f6371e76" :step/math-academy-id 22905 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121061 :tutorial/content "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{aligned}| & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n![](images/fe/fe02091dde43a8a2405f6b588f619bc9fa3f54b31ac8a1ac39d318761f00cc3e.svg)\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "1c99dfd1-b583-5247-9ac5-c2ada47c0ee9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3880 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix By a Scalar"} :step/id #uuid "ebb7b384-6a1f-56e3-9927-8911a4ec3563" :step/math-academy-id 22922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119666 :question/id #uuid "ce910ef2-c3af-5173-84d4-ee2a4c957954" :question/math-academy-id "e-6148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "272d9a54-ba97-5175-86df-0a78337da7b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440485}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Matrix by a Scalar"} :step/id #uuid "6bb5981c-60d1-56ed-a146-3ef4d9c0f6bb" :step/math-academy-id 22923 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120628}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119994}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409694} :activity/id #uuid "26bcedbd-f502-5256-b322-6d751a0c036d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08098a6b-f06b-51d7-be19-39ddeb566f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409658} {:answer/id #uuid "4da115ec-321e-53f5-9c9a-06b3818508a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409661} {:answer/id #uuid "7105bb69-b089-5e84-ab20-fd40c1edbdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-864" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409667} {:answer/id #uuid "b58e6bc8-29ce-5a41-8721-675b80eb22cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409673} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd30542-3cc5-5b61-a9af-49753027f933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409673} :answer-field/id #uuid "04759499-638f-5548-ad9a-51762b698235" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fded0a2c-cd1a-5de1-a671-20ea991daecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-72737" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(A)=-4.$ What is the value of $det(6A)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=6$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(6A) & ={6}^{2}\\cdot det(A) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-144.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0f93ad19-15c5-55b8-98ea-d5bcee8f0a3a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409696}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "403dcee3-3eac-5cf3-a329-0558750fb3b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409660} {:answer/id #uuid "62a1a85b-a36c-54a7-b3bd-55a36f4cb315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409664} {:answer/id #uuid "77636ea6-fba2-560e-a69f-f3d2282f86f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409669} {:answer/id #uuid "81cdf51b-4eb6-557c-9452-11d65bc919bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409671} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe826b2-b0aa-57e0-a4ad-251af663d3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409671} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a00ccd6-15ad-566e-9909-ced57101fb7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a80ad4c-5529-5931-9d7c-574d40c6f47e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72748" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(B)=-7.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}$ involves multiplying a row by $2,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $2$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $2$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =2\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-2det(B) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2aef95ee-e7e9-5cac-b9ad-3b67a53db79f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e01723b-cc89-5e3f-b933-6517b2094770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409662} {:answer/id #uuid "91ff05b8-c923-5cbc-9974-2858e86d4877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409672} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b3cea5-79ed-5121-8909-27b79eb945a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409674} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a98397-80e5-5a87-b4ed-d166d96908c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409675} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7338ba-732f-5bfb-aded-530b4a5f8d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409677} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc16361c-3306-5054-a003-e31388cab6e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a9d752b-69e4-5f21-888f-3429a8443f7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42503" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=12.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ and ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{3}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(12) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2df25dd7-50b3-560b-9abe-b6b13654a237" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409695}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5903e6-2dfb-5988-91aa-d76353ccd959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409659} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3a3be9-dab5-5f7f-8e95-4eab3b47ad8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409665} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7bf72b-a934-51fa-b417-3e7f04a3f3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409666} {:answer/id #uuid "7190fbd3-e0c2-5f59-a1f5-3e49c40c46d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=2{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409668} {:answer/id #uuid "c8afe3f3-a5ef-55a7-b5dd-30e21d2e58fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409659} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a779adf-221a-5189-a14f-312c86e15c0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ac571f6-6f05-5f70-8bcf-34738f544556" :question/math-academy-id "q-43046" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8e6fad07-ec42-5416-85eb-9ef2b0059cbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "512abaff-433d-5e4a-864f-f1df10d56d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409663} {:answer/id #uuid "7a56edfe-4461-57d1-8804-22f3e005401a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409670} {:answer/id #uuid "f689939f-47fe-5c00-a125-9fe1fc1187ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409679} {:answer/id #uuid "face2d4d-2a2b-5eb7-8047-8fb6dbd54d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409680} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf8031f-e31a-5703-96b3-5a28bec971aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409682}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409682} :answer-field/id #uuid "98c176ba-da66-551c-9c0c-0c257e4d9459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "81c5993c-3d71-5869-bbf5-3e36b2df3a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72741" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(Y)=5.$ What is the value of $det(2Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot Y)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=2$ and $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(2Y) & ={2}^{6}\\cdot det(Y) \\\\  & =64\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =320.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d75809ee-199d-50e2-b002-2326810f1174" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409693}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409657}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424902} :activity/id #uuid "11b6f742-f965-5993-a06f-bca8dfba7485" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5631a190-3164-5c81-9c22-e6a70c44c09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263742} {:answer/id #uuid "74e1b546-dc5d-5c39-8615-b03158b816ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264014} {:answer/id #uuid "850ef7c7-1f4b-53c9-b078-25bda4009629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264145} {:answer/id #uuid "df7141db-3b66-5a7a-b89e-874dcb0bbee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264937} {:answer/id #uuid "e044d557-77d9-53d0-a554-77b04c1a4a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264014} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf6cdcea-ffe0-5e06-a7c5-0284d3815f01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e099506-9b8a-52ad-a7eb-ff6f42b6d002" :question/math-academy-id "q-130855" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=5$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(5) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0950983d-5529-5332-ba75-7636f5397a6b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0f29de-25aa-5107-8046-0dd732f9beb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263097} {:answer/id #uuid "162deb0b-2730-571d-b037-7db47a3aea46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263209} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8f75e2-26ce-5967-ac8a-f06080423598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263259} {:answer/id #uuid "66de9e61-f678-5f09-92fa-20666c0a4a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263891} {:answer/id #uuid "f578aa5a-7ba5-5a91-8942-93f38512c9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263097} :answer-field/id #uuid "f00b119d-2bbd-5aff-88de-546820f3c832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da7f3dfc-3649-55d6-80af-942a6a83828d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130767" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(X)=28.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+(-7){R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{6}:={R}_{6}+(-7){R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{3}{4},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{3}{4}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{3}{4}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =\\frac{3}{4}det(X) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5a731d48-68b1-58c7-b15e-4a787d1f4b44" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329661 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038f903c-07cf-5804-8ccf-c48c4437fe4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326971} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5d0fbb-4c6d-516a-bbe1-bc9a4754768c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327766} {:answer/id #uuid "7edb508d-7daa-5a42-80ea-172f811c7a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328025} {:answer/id #uuid "cef987a3-28b5-50d6-be17-23ad82b7d421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328755} {:answer/id #uuid "dce0ee78-0312-5fae-84a0-aea62c27e409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328755} :answer-field/id #uuid "91964048-e6f4-5262-9e11-ecd0686eeeaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "44ea0623-3f8f-525c-8344-8d911fbef23b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42504" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{3}:=3{R}_{3}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:=3{R}_{3}$ involves multiplying a row by $3,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $3$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $3$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-3det(A) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5b098fcf-c1b8-5f75-8b3a-28dc83ab8997" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171230a8-065f-5312-91ff-166c6fb3df4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424893} {:answer/id #uuid "21bc26a0-57ad-5e62-a6e6-621e2ba24583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424894} {:answer/id #uuid "54d72f80-7a7b-5b93-a0ec-2d6edad08ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424895} {:answer/id #uuid "6c11e90f-184e-57bd-b3ed-1971d8fb82ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=-{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424896} {:answer/id #uuid "acde6755-f2db-5c6c-bdc1-ac03bdaa2931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424894} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75bea69-7db0-5820-97b1-b5dd95b8b845" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "200dace6-387b-586f-87ab-a5501c01cbe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-130851" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "796d84ed-30ec-5eef-9c9e-3ecd61007abc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17922ecf-87f8-5af9-872a-3d5dc6da26af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339339} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3f2587-b4ad-5bf0-81b7-1df62ffb7a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339686} {:answer/id #uuid "a21fdf0b-c950-5ec9-b67b-31a39827c266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340600} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1d7c41-28d1-55a3-9882-2c2b96500495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340714} {:answer/id #uuid "f3da6cbc-6c90-5ed2-9aad-88c3625d3d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341277} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded707a6-03c1-52cc-8868-8933afddd3df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bce6072f-0fd1-50b6-a305-a94b72b85f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72740" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(Y)=-3.$ What is the value of $det(4Y)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot Y)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(Y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(4Y) & ={4}^{3}\\cdot det(Y) \\\\  & =64\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-192.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e253f5b-694c-5716-b2be-ec3f736b8398" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424901}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424892}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440490} :activity/id #uuid "f4aebbc3-f038-5073-b26d-b3a33a4479cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462 :topic/id #uuid "a1bb4ea4-3502-539f-8843-4f544ac297ef" :topic/math-academy-id 1772} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "319beb45-da82-5928-83bf-d29bc52ed19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378659} {:answer/id #uuid "a259e746-f501-54ae-be55-fef569b47320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378660} {:answer/id #uuid "21d9eeeb-c150-51f2-9c34-5dfbb8074a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378661} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0902b3-371c-5ed1-bcca-bdf20d2b6da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378662} {:answer/id #uuid "6227f8a3-0c1e-5bad-a5c1-bba85528c3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378660} :answer-field/id #uuid "addc5023-5343-5d6e-94d4-459e9803d13f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbcc8a51-42ac-5cb8-a6bc-0647017d9ba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130858" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=14$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(14) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b56b3f3-0eed-5dd2-8e0f-81ffa9b89ff3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c504d34-49a8-5293-9654-9bb371251af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263118} {:answer/id #uuid "668e2da3-ac3d-5bec-b6b1-16504c2d81fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263886} {:answer/id #uuid "7573103e-73e5-56e6-832d-af6582780369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264021} {:answer/id #uuid "90344634-d8cd-51fb-85d7-7f08481a8bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236} {:answer/id #uuid "c168c56a-3c78-5c71-873d-48190da505aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236} :answer-field/id #uuid "37455212-8943-576e-a134-59b320488bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b71bc5d0-3557-578c-8a3d-25da59fcce16" :question/math-academy-id "q-130771" :question/problem "Suppose $B$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(B)=-75.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{2}$ and ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{6}.$ What is the value of $det(C)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{6}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{2}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{4}{5},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{4}{5}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $\\frac{4}{5}$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-det(B)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}det(B) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-75) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1776f775-68f2-5dfe-9a78-5fcd889a28cc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59b9b5a2-8dd1-5764-aa90-63de7756ab2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383218} {:answer/id #uuid "bea9ac5f-6965-5feb-9890-9a84bb8fa9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383219} {:answer/id #uuid "c930d2e4-f0f9-5e23-b593-fbc36215932d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383220} {:answer/id #uuid "dce12cc1-6fe9-5710-9501-662de6d6ea91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{3}:=-3{R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383221} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5d3036-4719-5fd7-ba58-918a06722081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383220} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2936d46-0370-550d-9d6e-65ba694a9efd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "38a459ba-c9b0-57c4-8ac3-81669bb1022e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130848" :question/problem "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bc80a516-1dfa-5188-a931-81163ba5b5d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440489 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017c23cc-c363-5c6e-87b5-6e7c3ae5e977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440472} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4fda87-7e6e-55cf-a40d-16eb860dbe92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440474} {:answer/id #uuid "81b440ea-43a7-5db4-bd71-d49d81ed4b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440477} {:answer/id #uuid "b26eabf3-1144-53cd-932b-1525e01f62fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440479} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7979e9-0f01-5714-b611-2b2303ead7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440474} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1483cc5-4c9b-5aca-8098-5a910582cb85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba8abd45-3c22-5be5-a8a7-0f2c616dbcc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-130770" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a $9\\times 9$ matrix and $det(Y)=75.$ The matrix $Z$ is obtained from $Y$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{8}↔{R}_{9}$ and ${R}_{7}:={R}_{7}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{2}.$ What is the value of $det(Z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{8}↔{R}_{9}$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{7}:={R}_{7}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{2}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we only have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Z) & =-det(Y) \\\\  & =-(75) \\\\  & =-75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dddee882-e3d5-5fc5-bf4f-fc432c1be973" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a794be-72e1-5850-a9e7-4f09532bb4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440473} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9a3462-424a-5e23-93a1-21351e2c8529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440475} {:answer/id #uuid "3aad9ad4-b303-5b2b-9d26-6d2c4b559298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440476} {:answer/id #uuid "8a223201-44a6-5017-aa80-a127d44a8e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-147" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440478} {:answer/id #uuid "c00986c8-2ab1-5ba7-aed5-8ac51531c5fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-294" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440478} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad94b206-3ccb-5817-8ec1-05230e22359f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc497788-364d-5c29-8657-a4f1556e2704" :question/math-academy-id "q-72744" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $det(X)=-3.$ What is the value of $det(7X)?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot X)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(X).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=7$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(7X) & ={7}^{2}\\cdot det(X) \\\\  & =49\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-147.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ea78220d-d076-5d61-804b-70c264ba0078" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440489}}] :activity/title "Row and Column Operations on Determinants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440471}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162974} :activity/id #uuid "9110c851-c32a-57df-a77a-329797a3df41" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731 :topic/id #uuid "b99b224f-8118-53d5-8d6e-c47785040b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162056 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161856 :question/id #uuid "01fc4e2e-d93f-50b3-859e-19732d4f10c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9111"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "405984cd-1e1b-54c6-bc75-268e127b9812" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435999}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Constant"} :step/id #uuid "3174d04a-7e8e-5098-91d3-c55afedd2e39" :step/math-academy-id 33959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162972 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162057 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161857 :question/id #uuid "a3f88c0e-9426-50d2-baf0-95909e222d4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a3752a0-431d-5520-97b1-493c36bbce54" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436001}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "fe3d0b73-cb7e-5370-9455-c490da334890" :step/math-academy-id 33961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162976}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162058 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161858 :question/id #uuid "c20c7b15-e967-52a9-846d-4915f6661158" :question/math-academy-id "e-9114"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436002}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "09f12104-7949-5867-9884-48e422a32857" :step/math-academy-id 33963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163273 :tutorial/content "There are several important properties of the variance of a random variable that we can use to simplify expressions. \n\nFirstly, the variance of a constant is always zero. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[a] = 0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this makes sense. A constant does not vary, so the mean deviation from its expected value should be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "6eb17ec7-1e57-5d75-8a71-9030a420602b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5458 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65bbda75-37ec-5809-926a-0fb8d31c008d" :step/math-academy-id 33964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162971}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163274 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ is a constant. Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-4X] & =(-4)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To understand why we have to square the constant when we factor it outside the variance, it's helpful to remember that the variance is defined as the expected *squared* deviation from the mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX] & =\\text{E}[(aX-\\text{E}[aX])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d33b6982-066e-5c82-8138-0283ad8dccbb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5459 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "55f9fd86-1f4a-5f2c-b7c2-a60113a4a9e8" :step/math-academy-id 33966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162972}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163275 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nA proof of this result is given at the end of this lesson.\n\nTherefore, when a random variable is scaled by a factor of $a$ and shifted by a constant $b\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- the variance is scaled by a factor of $a^2$ as before, and\n\n- the variance is unaffected by the shift.\n\nThe fact that shifting a random variable leaves the variance unaltered makes intuitive sense. Shifting all values of $X$ by a fixed amount does not change how far apart those values are from each other. \n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+1] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1523e51b-84da-5100-809f-09fbcb0d80ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5460 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e17beec2-91ed-5375-9c58-ab279784f2bf" :step/math-academy-id 33968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162973}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163276 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+b] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\nRecall that, by definition,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[aX+b] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we can simplify this as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(aX+b-\\text{E}[aX+b])^{2}] & =\\text{E}[(aX+b-a\\text{E}[X]-b)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(aX-a\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(a(X-\\text{E}[X]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{a}^{2}(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0372f0de-b6a6-58ee-8195-c04024e8f745" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5461 :tutorial/title "Proof of the General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "da23e59c-3020-5db2-8f5b-77675938bf0b" :step/math-academy-id 33969}] :activity/title "Properties of Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162181}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425510} :activity/id #uuid "6f0ea926-98c1-508c-aafd-6b9334df01ce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731 :topic/id #uuid "b99b224f-8118-53d5-8d6e-c47785040b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425507 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9447cf-647a-5fa5-b1b7-8ad767262b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425493} {:answer/id #uuid "26c878c9-4005-5ffb-b62a-ccf12e7653a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425495} {:answer/id #uuid "84d7e9cc-2b46-5e49-827d-3dc43ec7a3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425499} {:answer/id #uuid "a04fede6-c5e8-5346-92ee-0dfe50e7363c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425500} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e38c85-b6da-52ad-a873-fe8715cae083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425493} :answer-field/id #uuid "9be7f666-e571-52b8-923e-291edd79be24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2adf151d-6d7b-5aee-975a-4588dddc072c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109086" :question/problem "If $\\text{Var}[X]=18,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{3}X+1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $b=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{3}X+1] & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0925688f-b14f-5497-b2f8-ef5577ee74ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425511}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425508 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af8dce06-ec2e-54f0-8068-081d29a00d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158805} {:answer/id #uuid "f65b10d3-6570-596d-aef7-36a7d9e90bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158806} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c1bb8e-9e55-5585-849e-6e82c2d6778c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158807} {:answer/id #uuid "2866549e-9133-5ffd-b73b-2bb7a74f8397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158808} {:answer/id #uuid "4319aba1-1a6d-55f0-8649-0a11c8e26ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158808} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f5d0fa-e64f-5420-894c-9af20eb4994a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07dc96f1-44f9-56f8-847d-dfc0e5291318" :question/math-academy-id "q-108946" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=7,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[2-5X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-5$ and $b=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2-5X] & =(-5)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=7.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6)+{9}^{2}\\cdot f(9) \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{5})+36(\\frac{2}{5})+81(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}-49 \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2-5X] & =25\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =25\\cdot \\frac{14}{5} \\\\  & =14\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1bca5d11-c663-5970-a2d7-41fbabf9a6af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425509}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425509 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ffc334f-bda6-5020-8972-7c141e811580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425494} {:answer/id #uuid "2a01301e-2613-5381-a06c-9e16aa051fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425496} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1bb640-287c-5a4d-9809-a95f12e541cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425497} {:answer/id #uuid "559bb6ee-9e3b-59fc-b4ec-1934f445e465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425498} {:answer/id #uuid "bea01d67-a086-5031-9b05-53086e759f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425496} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef907e56-83d3-5832-9059-ba2b6e97be1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6da9aedf-d051-5c8d-9ea5-b796001b82b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109056" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X] & ={\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=2.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{3}X] & =3\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2775e72e-c277-5c4c-8f6b-b21b5d18a293" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425507}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425510 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdc2e9bd-883b-53b3-9944-cfc609ec7c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158787} {:answer/id #uuid "1da0e3f6-cfb8-53e6-a30c-128f6ea47744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158788} {:answer/id #uuid "456a4994-4a3b-5692-99e8-a647708d7a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158789} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc8dced-9c70-5f0e-a44a-554c2928171a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158790} {:answer/id #uuid "57a37707-7dd6-55aa-bf77-c3c838e9d2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158787} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a78dcdb-24b3-5b74-9b4c-2a39c0c6e272" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1375f84f-9c97-5d6b-9ab0-2b5843b7a89e" :question/math-academy-id "q-108936" :question/problem "If $\\text{Var}[X]=8,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[2X].$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53444b7f-e4a7-573c-87a7-c9522567fb10" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425508}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425511 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f0111e-003f-5538-a3b8-ed1430d08fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420111} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f2c3dd-df0a-57f1-9b19-b251ffe9a72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420122} {:answer/id #uuid "bc26c2f9-39d9-5339-a3e8-be5d2e945ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420123} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d13b79-c01f-5ddb-888f-f826b5a1451c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420124} {:answer/id #uuid "e83e0132-215d-5133-98be-6554e8873e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420128} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0a832f-24dd-571f-b252-8ec9cfdfbd85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12feee0e-318a-5930-8eca-dd111ec7b1c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109091" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=1,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{1}{8}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X] & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=1.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}X] & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e8ab5da7-93a7-56c3-964a-5c487de36ed5"}] :activity/title "Properties of Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425492}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435373} :activity/id #uuid "25dbc02e-bef9-5aef-9959-0f81fd1d0280" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731 :topic/id #uuid "b99b224f-8118-53d5-8d6e-c47785040b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "643b59ab-e862-5975-86dd-7355eed4cca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242081} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1ac25e-ba63-509b-9370-23e96efe4ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242163} {:answer/id #uuid "bac7e8e7-11c4-554e-b61e-02f375d7783a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242819} {:answer/id #uuid "dc315568-edd6-5abe-b917-d1c33d5e4173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243136} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe8786-4596-56fa-ad58-012b52d63167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243186} :answer-field/id #uuid "718f5cfd-3b86-5662-9124-8b1ae998dc61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7bc237e8-7011-5d1b-897c-e565fd160016" :question/math-academy-id "q-109090" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02fca583-999d-5fea-95bb-2fa89ae7e5ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435374}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039f84c9-15b6-5535-9aa7-17f63520b5ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435365} {:answer/id #uuid "31713117-0676-52eb-93ae-63c695655871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435366} {:answer/id #uuid "7d294bd2-f457-5854-b801-d01d068966a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435367} {:answer/id #uuid "a9534937-1eca-5636-899b-550bfbfe6e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435368} {:answer/id #uuid "fc28d66e-a0b9-5d76-9c20-a4f103450187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435367} :answer-field/id #uuid "aadf43d0-7c01-553a-93ba-140bd9fcc568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a7e780b8-35dc-5693-8651-c458793347c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-109059" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=1,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[-2X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-2X] & =(-2)^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=1.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[-2X] & =4\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "142fd15b-c875-518a-9165-bb478aa0e810" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435374 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a7a743c-110e-5e17-9b8e-c0a4ed4bf822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369653} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bc9a45-3558-56aa-b44c-19b91bed820a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369654} {:answer/id #uuid "6840aa01-0203-5583-a1fb-3ee0ba2fced3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369655} {:answer/id #uuid "53b84c0f-bed5-541b-8d3e-723a5410d69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369656} {:answer/id #uuid "288b9421-5232-599d-975c-4cacf3b01709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369657}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369655} :answer-field/id #uuid "e907f6da-cd04-5106-a3c9-cf468f523d2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aeeea4a5-1443-5d67-a950-8990e0faa656" :question/math-academy-id "q-129386" :question/problem "If $\\text{Var}[X]=3.6,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\frac{10}{3}X].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{10}{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\frac{10}{3}X] & ={(\\frac{10}{3})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{9}\\cdot 3.6 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "98fbfae8-3ff5-5189-8ae4-c342310ec549"}] :activity/title "Properties of Variance for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id